Interview, Jan 2026, "Racing Innovators" Magazine.\
Interviewer: Welcome, Gordy! It\'92s a pleasure to have you with us at Racing Innovators. Today, I'd like to start by diving into the world of team formation and configuration in racing. From your extensive experience, what does the optimal team look like?\
\
\
Gordy: Thanks for having me! When it comes to team formation, the most important thing is that people are invested and communicating frequently. Everyone needs to know their job and be passionate about it. However, there\'92s no one-size-fits-all approach. Each team has its own dynamics and ways of organizing, and that's perfectly fine. As long as everyone is committed and knows their responsibilities, the structure can vary greatly and still be successful.\
\
\
Interviewer: That\'92s an interesting take. But, considering your experience, can you share any unconventional team structures that worked particularly well?\
\
\
Gordy: Sure. I remember working with a team, Team Thunder, that implemented a rather unique structure. We actually ran two sub-teams within the main team. These sub-teams would compete against each other before we finalized our strategy for the race. This internal competition was incredibly beneficial. It allowed us to experiment with different approaches and take advantage of new technologies. When prediction and sample splitting came along in the recent years, it was a game changer. We could test more variables and scenarios in a controlled environment, leading to more informed decisions on race day. This approach might not work for everyone, but it was a great fit for us and really pushed our performance to new heights.\
\
\
Interviewer: That\'92s a fascinating strategy. Speaking of data and technology, there must be a lot of data collected these days during practice and race sessions. How can a team effectively incorporate practice and race data into their analyses?\
\
\
Gordy: Incorporating those types of data into analyses isn\'92t all that different from working with basic simulator data. The key is to keep feeding the machines with as much data as possible. This consistent input helps in refining the models and improving predictions. However, it\'92s not just about quantity; you need to look for the gold nuggets within that data. These insights can often be hidden, but when you find them, they can provide a significant competitive edge. It\'92s about striking a balance between automated data processing and human analysis to uncover actionable insights.\
\
\
Interviewer: It sounds like technology has really transformed the way teams operate. What are some of the most significant changes you\'92ve seen in recent years?\
\
\
Gordy: Absolutely, technology has revolutionized racing. The most significant change is the use of advanced data analytics and machine learning. Teams can now predict outcomes with greater accuracy and tailor their strategies accordingly. However, there's running models and then there's deciding what to do with the models. My model says my lap time will be two minutes. Great. Where do I go from there? These days you need to be the best with models and the best with decision-making. No team can win without the two aspects.\
\
\
Interviewer: With all these advances, how do you see the future of racing evolving?\
\
\
Gordy: The future of racing is incredibly exciting. I see further integration of AI and machine learning, making predictions even more accurate and strategies more refined. Autonomous testing could become a norm, where cars test themselves under different conditions to provide data. We might also see more real-time adjustments during races, with teams being able to make split-second decisions based on live data feeds. It seems the sport is thinking of reintroducing active suspension, which would open up the scope for dynamic adjustments throughout races. Sustainability will also play a big role, with technology driving more eco-friendly racing solutions. Overall, the blend of human skill and advanced technology will continue to push the boundaries of what\'92s possible in racing.\
\
\
Interviewer: Gordy, it\'92s been enlightening to hear your insights on team formation, data integration, and the future of racing. Thank you for sharing your experiences with Racing Innovators.\
\
\
Gordy: My pleasure! It\'92s always great to discuss the evolving world of racing. Thanks for having me.\
\
\
Gordon "Gordy" MacMillan	Racing Veteran; Racecar Setup Specialist;\
Team Principal; Author of "The Racing Line"\
\
Interview, March 2016, "The Apex" Magazine.\
Interviewer: Welcome, Gordy! It\'92s an honor to have you with us at The Apex. You\'92ve had such a legendary career across multiple teams and championships, having directed three different teams to the championship win. Let\'92s start by talking about your time with Team Lightning during the late 90s. What were some of the key strategies and experiences that defined your success with them?\
\
\
Gordy: Thanks for having me! Team Lightning, those were some unforgettable years. Lots of things changed in the 90s for racing, but perhaps the one with the most impact was our novel approach to fuel management. We had this unconventional strategy where we'd run our fuel tanks to the very edge before pitting. Now, most people don't believe me to this day, but when your tank hits zero, there's usually just enough fuel left to finish the lap. It sounds risky, but if you plan to pit at that exact moment, you can push the car harder than the competition, knowing you'll pit at the end of the lap. Golly, it will run on fumes without losing a second. I remember the final race of the '98 championship. We were neck and neck with the leading team and needed the win. We timed our pit stop perfectly, planning the last lap with the minimum amount of fuel possible. It was nerve-wracking but gave us that crucial edge to secure the win and the championship! Timing is everything, and misjudging your pit lap can cost you the race, but when it works, it's magic. If the car doesn't have enough fuel to finish the lap, it will pit, but you shouldn't assume you'll lose the performance, especially with today's engines.\
\
\
Interviewer: That\'92s fascinating! Now, one of the topics I'd like to make sure we touch on is your championship with Team Thunder in early 2001. What had changed from the 90s and how did you manage the lead so early on?\
\
\
Gordy: Team Thunder was a different beast altogether; a very large structure... more resources. The early 2000s were all about understanding the nuances of car setup. More important than learning what to put in, it was all about what we could get out. Many believed that every little factor affected every possible setup decision, but that\'92s not entirely true. We learned to focus on the critical aspects that mattered most and treated each decision independently. One thing we did was to debunk the myth that setup should be changed according to fuel load or the tyres. We always optimized each decision separately, ensuring that each part of the car was at its best without unnecessary compromises. I remember the 2001 season as if it were today; we had a setup that was so finely tuned, it felt like we were gliding on the track. We didn\'92t waste time on connections that didn\'92t matter. That focus and clarity in decision-making were crucial for our unprecedented championship lead and victory.\
\
\
Interviewer: Over your career, you\'92ve led teams with some of the most iconic liveries. How much did those matter?\
\
\
Gordy: You know, I\'92ve heard that question a lot. The truth is, racing colors don\'92t make the car go any faster. It\'92s the decisions behind the scenes that do. The livery is great for team identity and sponsors, but when it comes to speed, it\'92s all about making good decisions in car setup, pit stop timing, and race strategy that really make the difference. I remember a race with Team Lightning where we changed our livery: The car looked stunning and we ended last. A good piece of advice is to focus all the energy on what matters. Learning what matters is where it's at.\
\
\
Interviewer: Just before the interview you were mentioning the importance of practice laps. During your time with various teams, how did you approach practice sessions?\
\
\
Gordy: Practice laps are gold. With every team I raced for, we treated practice sessions as our most valuable resource. They\'92re limited, so we had to be strategic about how we used them. The key wasn\'92t just running laps but understanding what each lap told us about the car\'92s performance and potential adjustments. We focused on gathering as much useful data as possible, which allowed us to make informed decisions come race day. During my stint with Team Lightning, we meticulously planned our practice sessions to test different setups and strategies. It wasn\'92t just about finding the fastest lap time but about understanding how different setups performed under various conditions. This attention to detail often gave us a significant edge when it mattered most. Ah... but you'll never guess what the most important thing in taking the car out to the track is...\
\
\
Interviewer: Will you tell me?\
\
\
Gordy: Sure... You need to first learn how much you can learn from practice. Forget that and the practice session is gone before you know it.\
\
\
Interviewer: Racing has changed significantly over the years. With the advancements in technology, how has simulator data impacted modern racing strategies?\
\
\
Gordy: Technology has revolutionized the sport. When I was with Team Fusion, we saw a massive shift towards using simulator data to inform our strategies. These days, junior analysts can extract incredible insights from what they call training data. It\'92s almost like having a crystal ball. Simulators allow us to test countless scenarios and setups before even hitting the track. Back in 2010, our data analysts identified patterns and trends that we hadn\'92t noticed before. This gave us a huge advantage in both car setup and race strategy. It\'92s amazing how far we\'92ve come, and utilizing this data effectively can be the difference between a good team and a great one.\
\
\
Interviewer: With advances in AI, it sounds like you can automate the whole thing...\
\
\
Gordy: You'd think! There's still no replacement for a sharp eye and attention to detail.\
\
\
Interviewer: One last question: Winning means staying ahead of the pack, and you've been able to do that probably more than anyone in this business. How much was that you, and how much was that coming from lessons from your rivals?\
\
\
Gordy: Monitoring rivals is crucial. I've always spent a long time watching race replays. You can learn a lot by analyzing how other teams handle different situations. We might not see everything, but it turns out identifying key moments is more informative than watching the whole rest of the race. In my junior years I'd win or lose races without knowing why. Today, show me a race replay and I can tell you exactly why a team outperformed another one.\
\
\
Interviewer: Gordy, it\'92s been an absolute pleasure hearing your stories and insights. Thank you for sharing your experiences with The Apex.\
\
\
Gordy: My pleasure! It\'92s always great to look back and share what I\'92ve learned. Thanks for having me.}